SAT_CHUNK("rw/form-structure/3/0",[
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.ffe1f38f5b","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-24","passage":"<p>The collection of letters, diaries, and photographs that researchers recovered from the abandoned lighthouse ______ a detailed picture of the keepers' isolated lives in the 1890s.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["have provided","provides","are providing","provide"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The subject is the singular \"collection,\" not the nearby plural nouns \"letters, diaries, and photographs\" or \"researchers.\" So the verb must be singular: \"provides.\" The other choices are plural."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.cc44ac3976","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-25","passage":"<p>Each of the three bridges that span the river near the old mill ______ been repaired at least twice since the flood of 1936.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["has","are","have","were"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The subject is \"Each,\" which is singular, so the verb must be singular: \"has.\" The plural words \"bridges\" and \"span\" come in between but don't change the subject. \"have,\" \"are,\" and \"were\" are plural or don't form the correct present perfect."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.7b6b39ee92","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-26","passage":"<p>Among the most surprising findings of the decade-long study ______ that people who lived near parks reported greater life satisfaction regardless of how often they visited them.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["were the discovery","being the discovery","was the discovery","have been the discovery"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The sentence is inverted: the subject \"the discovery\" follows the verb. Because \"discovery\" is singular, the verb must be \"was.\" The plural \"findings\" is part of a prepositional phrase and isn't the subject. \"being the discovery\" leaves the sentence without a main verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.de44397595","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-27","passage":"<p>The team of engineers, along with several consultants from the transportation department, ______ recommended that the city replace the aging bridge rather than repair it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["are","were","have","has"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The subject is \"team,\" a singular collective noun. The phrase \"along with several consultants\" doesn't make the subject plural, so the verb must be singular: \"has.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.7949235598","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-28","passage":"<p>Although the Parthenon has stood for nearly 2,500 years, much of the damage to ______ occurred in a single day in 1687, when a Venetian cannonball ignited gunpowder stored inside.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["their structure","its structure","they're structure","it's structure"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The pronoun refers to the singular \"Parthenon,\" so the singular possessive \"its\" is correct. \"their\" is plural. \"it's\" means \"it is,\" and \"they're\" means \"they are.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.380c663b37","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-29","passage":"<p>When the Ming dynasty's admiral Zheng He returned from his voyages across the Indian Ocean, ______ brought back giraffes, spices, and ambassadors from distant kingdoms.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["it","one","his fleet","they"],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"they\" and \"it\" have no clear antecedent (the dynasty? the voyages? the kingdoms?), and \"one\" is vague. Naming \"his fleet\" makes clear what brought back the goods."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.a03643f351","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-30","passage":"<p>By the time the museum opened its new wing in 2019, curators ______ more than 400 artifacts that had been kept in storage for decades.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["restore","have restored","had restored","will restore"],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"By the time... opened... in 2019\" sets up an action completed before another past action, which calls for the past perfect: \"had restored.\" \"restore\" is present tense, \"have restored\" connects to the present, and \"will restore\" is future."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.e5dbb3768f","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-31","passage":"<p>Having studied the migration patterns of monarch butterflies for more than twenty years, ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["the protection of the forests in central Mexico where monarchs spend the winter became a cause for entomologist Lincoln Brower.","the forests in central Mexico where monarchs spend the winter were championed by entomologist Lincoln Brower.","entomologist Lincoln Brower's advocacy helped protect the forests in central Mexico where the butterflies spend the winter.","entomologist Lincoln Brower became a leading advocate for protecting the forests in central Mexico where the butterflies spend the winter."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The introductory phrase \"Having studied...\" must describe the person who studied, so \"entomologist Lincoln Brower\" has to come right after the comma. The other choices place \"the forests,\" \"the protection,\" or \"Brower's advocacy\" there, which creates dangling modifiers."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.8b0ed34344","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-32","passage":"<p>The poet's early notebooks, which scholars ______ until 2004, reveal that she revised some poems more than thirty times before publishing them.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["have not examined","do not examine","had not examined","will not have examined"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The notebooks weren't examined until a past point (2004), so the action before that time takes the past perfect: \"had not examined.\" The present perfect \"have not examined\" conflicts with \"until 2004,\" and \"do not\" and \"will not have\" don't fit the past timeline."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.212400f621","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-33","passage":"<p>Studies show that octopuses, unlike most invertebrates, ______ able to use tools, solve puzzles, and recognize individual humans.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["to be","are","being","is"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The subject of \"that\" clause is \"octopuses,\" which is plural, so the verb is \"are.\" \"is\" is singular. \"being\" and \"to be\" aren't finite verbs, so the clause would lack a main verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.24721d6f40","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-34","passage":"<p>The researchers hoped that by tracking thousands of seabirds with small GPS tags, they could determine where the birds ______ during the months they spent away from their breeding colonies.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["feeding","fed","have fed","will feed"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The sentence describes past events (\"hoped,\" \"spent\"), so the verb in the clause should also be past tense: \"fed.\" \"feeding\" isn't a finite verb, so the clause \"where the birds feeding\" lacks a verb. \"will feed\" is future, and \"have fed\" doesn't fit the past context."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.c6155d5a70","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-35","passage":"<p>In 1960, the chemist Willard Libby received the Nobel Prize for developing radiocarbon dating, a method that ______ scientists to estimate the age of organic materials up to about 50,000 years old.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["allow","allows","allowing","have allowed"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The subject of the relative clause is \"a method,\" which is singular, and the method still works today, so the present-tense singular \"allows\" is correct. \"allow\" and \"have allowed\" are plural. \"allowing\" leaves the clause without a finite verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.641d34df08","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-36","passage":"<p>Neither the city council nor the residents of the neighborhood ______ in favor of building the proposed parking garage on the site of the old market.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["is","were","was","has been"],"answer":"B","explanation":"With \"neither... nor,\" the verb agrees with the nearer subject, \"residents,\" which is plural. So the plural \"were\" is correct. \"was,\" \"is,\" and \"has been\" are singular."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.56b238a4c2","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-37","passage":"<p>The Aztec capital of Tenochtitlan, with its canals, floating gardens, and markets that drew tens of thousands of traders each day, ______ astonished the Spanish soldiers who arrived there in 1519.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["were reported to have","reportedly were","reportedly","having reportedly"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The subject is the singular \"Tenochtitlan,\" and the main verb is \"astonished.\" Adding \"reportedly\" keeps \"astonished\" as the verb, and the sentence is complete. \"were reported to have\" is plural and ungrammatical here (\"have astonished\"). \"reportedly were\" produces \"were astonished the soldiers,\" which is incorrect. \"having reportedly\" leaves the sentence without a main verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.2c2912556b","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-38","passage":"<p>Scientists studying ice cores from Antarctica can measure the carbon dioxide trapped in tiny air bubbles, ______ how atmospheric conditions have changed over hundreds of thousands of years.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["revealing","reveals","they reveal","and revealing"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The phrase after the comma describes the result of measuring the carbon dioxide, so the participle \"revealing\" is correct. \"and revealing\" joins a participle to the main verb \"can measure\" as if they were parallel, which is ungrammatical. \"they reveal\" creates a comma splice. \"reveals\" has no subject."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.8071263a54","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-39","passage":"<p>The number of students enrolling in computer science courses at the university ______ tripled over the past decade, prompting administrators to hire additional faculty.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["are","have","has","were"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The subject is \"The number,\" which is singular. The plural nouns \"students\" and \"courses\" are in phrases that modify it. So the singular \"has\" is correct."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.0a5362477b","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-40","passage":"<p>The painter Jacob Lawrence completed his sixty-panel Migration Series in 1941; in these panels, ______ the movement of African Americans from the rural South to northern cities.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["depicting","he depicted","which depicted","having depicted"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The clause after the semicolon must be independent, with its own subject and verb: \"he depicted.\" \"depicting,\" \"which depicted,\" and \"having depicted\" leave the second part without a subject and main verb, which makes it a fragment."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.df2091da10","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-41","passage":"<p>Scholars have long debated whether the plays attributed to Shakespeare were written by him alone; most now agree that he collaborated with other playwrights on several of ______ works.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["they're","his","him","its"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The possessive \"his\" refers to Shakespeare. \"him\" is an object pronoun and can't show possession. \"its\" has no singular nonhuman antecedent, and \"they're\" means \"they are.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.19192c2d10","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-42","passage":"<p>The villagers' tradition of weaving baskets from sweetgrass, passed from parents to children for generations, ______ recognized in 2006 as an important part of the region's cultural heritage.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["was","being","were","have been"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The subject is \"tradition,\" which is singular, so the verb must be \"was.\" \"villagers'\" and \"baskets\" aren't the subject. \"were\" and \"have been\" are plural, and \"being\" isn't a finite verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.1024b6124d","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-43","passage":"<p>In 1848, gold was discovered at Sutter's Mill in California. Within a year, tens of thousands of prospectors ______ to the region, transforming San Francisco from a small settlement into a bustling city.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["have flocked","flock","had flocked","will flock"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The migration happened within a year after 1848 and before the city's transformation was complete, so a past form is needed. \"had flocked\" correctly places the action in the past before a later past point. \"flock,\" \"have flocked,\" and \"will flock\" are present, present perfect, and future."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.a41f1c324e","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-44","passage":"<p>Though the two species of finches look nearly identical, ______ songs differ enough that females of each species can easily recognize potential mates.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["there","they're","their","its"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The pronoun refers to \"the two species of finches,\" which is plural, so \"their\" is correct. \"its\" is singular, \"they're\" means \"they are,\" and \"there\" indicates a place."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.d7372bf00b","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-45","passage":"<p>The discovery of penicillin, along with the development of other antibiotics in the following decades, ______ the treatment of bacterial infections.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["transformed","were transforming","have transformed","transforming"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The subject is the singular \"discovery\"; \"along with the development...\" doesn't make it plural. The past-tense \"transformed\" works with any number and fits the historical context. \"were transforming\" and \"have transformed\" are plural, and \"transforming\" leaves the sentence without a main verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.a0b77d55a2","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data5-46","passage":"<p>Before the invention of the printing press, books had to be copied by hand, a process that was so slow that a single copy of the Bible could take a scribe more than a year ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["to complete.","in completion of.","completing.","to be completing."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"take... to complete\" is the standard idiom: an infinitive follows \"take [time].\" \"completing\" and \"in completion of\" don't fit the construction, and \"to be completing\" is an unnecessary and awkward progressive infinitive."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.2ea6f8f7a4","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-20","passage":"<p>A series of experiments conducted by researchers in three countries ______ that the vaccine remains effective for at least ten years.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["are suggesting","suggests","have suggested","suggest"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The subject is \"A series,\" which is singular, so the verb must be singular: \"suggests.\" The plural nouns \"experiments,\" \"researchers,\" and \"countries\" are in modifying phrases. \"suggest,\" \"are suggesting,\" and \"have suggested\" are plural."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.77bee1b8d1","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-21","passage":"<p>The recipes in the eighteenth-century cookbook, handwritten by several generations of the same family, ______ ingredients such as rosewater, mace, and saffron that are rarely used in modern kitchens.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["is calling for","has called for","calls for","call for"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The subject is \"recipes,\" which is plural, so the verb must be plural: \"call for.\" \"cookbook\" and \"family\" aren't the subject. The other choices are singular."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.977aab83b9","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-22","passage":"<p>Once considered a nuisance, the zebra mussel, along with several other invasive species, ______ now being studied for ways it might filter pollutants from lakes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["is","were","have been","are"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The subject is \"the zebra mussel,\" which is singular. \"along with several other invasive species\" doesn't make it plural. The singular \"is\" fits the present-tense \"now.\" \"are,\" \"were,\" and \"have been\" are plural."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.0656febc1f","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-23","passage":"<p>Before she became a celebrated poet, Gwendolyn Brooks ______ poems to a Chicago newspaper for years, publishing more than seventy of them in a weekly column.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["contributes","has contributed","will contribute","had contributed"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The contributions happened before she became celebrated, a past point, so the past perfect \"had contributed\" is correct. \"contributes\" is present, \"will contribute\" is future, and \"has contributed\" doesn't fit a sequence of past events."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.5e9ab0e503","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-24","passage":"<p>The committee members disagreed about many details of the proposal, but each of them ______ that the city's aging water system needed urgent repairs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["acknowledged","acknowledge","have acknowledged","are acknowledging"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The subject \"each\" is singular, and the sentence is in the past tense (\"disagreed\"), so \"acknowledged\" is correct. \"acknowledge\" and \"have acknowledged\" are plural forms, and \"are acknowledging\" is plural and present."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.d55ab649d0","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-25","passage":"<p>Many ______ ideas about the formation of the solar system were revised after telescopes detected planets orbiting other stars.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["astronomers'","astronomers's","astronomer's","astronomers"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The ideas belong to many astronomers, so the plural possessive \"astronomers'\" is correct. \"astronomer's\" is singular. \"astronomers\" is plural but not possessive. \"astronomers's\" is not a standard form."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.2abf19e64e","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-26","passage":"<p>The documentary follows a family of cheetahs across the Serengeti, capturing the mother's efforts to protect ______ cubs from lions and hyenas.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["they're","their","hers","her"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The pronoun refers to \"the mother,\" a single female cheetah, so the possessive \"her\" is correct. \"their\" is plural. \"hers\" is a possessive pronoun that stands alone and can't come before a noun. \"they're\" means \"they are.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.d9f73a6567","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-27","passage":"<p>Walking through the ancient city of Petra, ______</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["the rose-colored sandstone cliffs have buildings carved directly into them.","visitors can see buildings carved directly into the rose-colored sandstone cliffs.","the buildings that visitors see are carved directly into the rose-colored sandstone cliffs.","buildings carved directly into the rose-colored sandstone cliffs can be seen by visitors."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The introductory phrase \"Walking through the ancient city of Petra\" must describe the people walking, so \"visitors\" must come right after the comma. The other choices place \"the cliffs\" or \"the buildings\" there, creating dangling modifiers."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.0812f7d45d","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-28","passage":"<p>By the end of the nineteenth century, railroads ______ the travel time between New York and Chicago from several weeks to less than a day.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["will have reduced","had reduced","are reducing","reduce"],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"By the end of the nineteenth century\" marks a past point by which the action was complete, so the past perfect \"had reduced\" is correct. \"reduce\" and \"are reducing\" are present, and \"will have reduced\" is future."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.90dfadd725","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-29","passage":"<p>The measurements collected by the two satellites ______ that sea levels have risen by about ten centimeters since 1993.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["has shown","show","is showing","shows"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The subject is \"measurements,\" which is plural, so the verb must be plural: \"show.\" \"satellites\" is in a modifying phrase. \"shows,\" \"has shown,\" and \"is showing\" are singular."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.de06a3efad","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-30","passage":"<p>Rarely ______ a single discovery change an entire field as dramatically as the discovery of DNA's structure changed biology.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["do","did it","does","it does"],"answer":"C","explanation":"After a negative adverb like \"Rarely\" at the start of a clause, the subject and auxiliary verb are inverted: \"Rarely does a single discovery change...\". \"do\" doesn't agree with the singular \"discovery.\" \"did it\" adds an unnecessary pronoun, and \"it does\" doesn't invert the order and adds an extra subject."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.da7e0ea048","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-31","passage":"<p>The ecologist's goal was not only to count the wolves in the park but also ______ how their presence affected elk behavior.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["understanding","to understand","understood","she understood"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The paired construction \"not only... but also\" requires parallel forms: \"to count\" is paired with \"to understand.\" \"understanding,\" \"she understood,\" and \"understood\" break the parallel structure."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.225aec7d63","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-32","passage":"<p>Scientists have found that octopuses, which have about 500 million neurons, ______ most of those neurons in their arms rather than in their central brain.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["has","to have","have","having"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The subject is \"octopuses,\" which is plural, so the verb must be \"have.\" \"has\" is singular. \"having\" and \"to have\" aren't finite verbs, which leaves the clause without a verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.73042bb8fb","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-33","passage":"<p>If the city ______ the bridge in 2010 as engineers recommended, the recent repairs would not have been necessary.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["had replaced","would replace","replaced","has replaced"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The sentence describes a condition contrary to past fact, so it needs the past perfect in the \"if\" clause to match \"would not have been\": \"had replaced.\" \"replaced\" is simple past, \"has replaced\" is present perfect, and \"would replace\" belongs in the result clause, not the condition."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.6e2bf6b10d","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-34","passage":"<p>In her memoir, the architect recalls how she and her colleagues ______ late into the night, sketching dozens of versions of the museum's curving roof.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["works","are working","worked","work"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The memoir recalls past events, so the past tense \"worked\" is correct and agrees with the compound subject \"she and her colleagues.\" \"work\" and \"are working\" are present tense, and \"works\" is singular and present."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.10c61cf1b7","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-35","passage":"<p>The number of bird species that live in the park year-round ______ by nearly a third since the wetlands were restored.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["are increasing","has increased","increase","have increased"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The subject is \"The number,\" which is singular, so the verb must be singular: \"has increased.\" \"species\" is in a modifying phrase. \"have increased,\" \"are increasing,\" and \"increase\" are plural."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.cf26b10aec","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-36","passage":"<p>Each of the novel's three sections is narrated by a different character, and ______ describes the same summer from a strikingly different perspective.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["each","they each","they","all"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The clause needs a singular subject that agrees with \"describes\": \"each.\" \"they each,\" \"all,\" and \"they\" are plural subjects that would require \"describe.\""},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.7335502fe2","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-37","passage":"<p>The painter's use of bright, unmixed colors, combined with her loose brushstrokes, ______ critics in the 1870s, many of whom dismissed the work as unfinished.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["shocking","have shocked","were shocking","shocked"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The subject is \"use,\" which is singular; \"combined with her loose brushstrokes\" doesn't make it plural. The past tense \"shocked\" fits the 1870s context and works with a singular subject. \"were shocking\" and \"have shocked\" are plural, and \"shocking\" leaves the sentence without a main verb."},
{"id":"g.rw.form-structure.3.05bc4fadd7","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"form-structure","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data6-38","passage":"<p>Among the artifacts recovered from the shipwreck ______ a bronze astrolabe, a navigational instrument used to determine latitude.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["were","are","was","have been"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The sentence is inverted, and the subject is \"a bronze astrolabe,\" which is singular, so the verb is \"was.\" \"artifacts\" is in a prepositional phrase and isn't the subject. \"were,\" \"have been,\" and \"are\" are plural."}
]);
